{:edb/db-before-t 12453 :edb/db-after-t 12454 :edb/tx-data [[13194139545766 50 #inst "2026-11-03T19:30:47.007Z" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186055014 1074 "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186055014 1074 "Implicit differentiation gives the tangent slope $-x/(4y)=-1/(2\\sqrt2)$ at $(\\sqrt2,1)$. The normal slope is its negative reciprocal, $2\\sqrt2$. Thus the normal is $y-1=2\\sqrt2(x-\\sqrt2)$. Setting $y=0$ yields $$x=\\sqrt2-\\frac1{2\\sqrt2}=\\frac3{2\\sqrt2}.$$ Therefore $P=\\left(\\tfrac3{2\\sqrt2},0\\right)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186055017 1074 "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=(0,\\tfrac{10}3)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186055017 1074 "Differentiating gives $6x-4y\\,y'=0$, hence the tangent slope at $(2,2)$ is $3/2$. The normal slope is $-2/3$, so its equation is $y-2=-\\tfrac23(x-2)$. At $x=0$, $y=2+\\tfrac43=\\tfrac{10}3$. Thus $P=\\left(0,\\tfrac{10}3\\right)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186092597 1074 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186092597 1074 "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. With $r=1$ and $\\theta=5\\pi/3$, the coordinates are $\\left(\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}2\\right)$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186092858 1074 "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52).$$" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186092858 1074 "With $r=5$ and $\\theta=330^\\circ$, $$P=(5\\cos330^\\circ,5\\sin330^\\circ)=\\left(\\tfrac{5\\sqrt3}2,-\\tfrac52\\right).$$" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186097161 1074 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\langle4,\\frac32\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186097161 1074 "The scale shows that two grid squares represent one unit. Reading the displacements from the diagram gives\n\n$$\nq=\\left\\langle\\frac52,-\\frac32\\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad r=\\left\\langle\\frac32,3\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $q+r=\\left\\langle4,\\frac32\\right\\rangle$ and\n\n$$\n|q+r|=\\sqrt{4^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}2.\n$$\n\nThe required unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\frac{q+r}{|q+r|}=\\frac{2(q+r)}{\\sqrt{73}}.\n$$" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186405534 1074 "Using the worksheet’s geometric meaning of integral curve, draw the entire right branch $x^2-y^2=1/4$, $x>0$, including its turn at $(1/2,0)$. One parametrization is $(x,y)=(\\tfrac12\\cosh s,\\tfrac12\\sinh s)$. Draw its visible portion within the supplied axes. Away from $y=0$, its slope is $dy/dx=\\cosh s/\\sinh s=x/y$. The whole curve is not a solution graph $y(x)$: for $x>1/2$ it has two $y$ values, and the explicit ODE is undefined at its vertical tangent $(1/2,0)$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/1a/1a426ad2bc023da1fc079d9ca7017c8735d1495114389a0d020cf1874195071d.png)\n\nHere “integral curve” means a geometric trajectory following the line directions, including a vertical turn; it is broader than a classical solution graph of $y\\prime=x/y$. The parametric tangent $(\\tfrac12\\sinh s,\\tfrac12\\cosh s)=(y,x)$ remains nonzero at the turn. Each upper/lower portion can separately define a solution for $x>1/2$, but their union cannot." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186405534 1074 "Using the worksheet’s geometric meaning of integral curve, draw the entire right branch $x^2-y^2=1/4$, $x>0$, including its turn at $(1/2,0)$. One parametrization is $(x,y)=\\left(\\tfrac12\\cosh s,\\tfrac12\\sinh s\\right)$. Draw its visible portion within the supplied axes. Away from $y=0$, its slope is $dy/dx=\\cosh s/\\sinh s=x/y$. The whole curve is not a solution graph $y(x)$: for $x>1/2$ it has two $y$ values, and the explicit ODE is undefined at its vertical tangent $(1/2,0)$.\n\n![Authored reference graph](images/1a/1a426ad2bc023da1fc079d9ca7017c8735d1495114389a0d020cf1874195071d.png)\n\nHere “integral curve” means a geometric trajectory following the line directions, including a vertical turn; it is broader than a classical solution graph of $y\\prime=x/y$. The parametric tangent $\\left(\\tfrac12\\sinh s,\\tfrac12\\cosh s\\right)=(y,x)$ remains nonzero at the turn. Each upper/lower portion can separately define a solution for $x>1/2$, but their union cannot." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186484536 1074 "For nonzero position vectors, $\\mathbf F=\\nabla[\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)]$. The fundamental theorem gives work $\\tfrac12\\ln84-\\tfrac12\\ln3=\\tfrac12\\ln28$. The field is undefined at the origin; any admissible path avoids it. No particular path is needed." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186484536 1074 "For nonzero position vectors, $\\mathbf F=\\nabla\\left[\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2+z^2)\\right]$. The fundamental theorem gives work $\\tfrac12\\ln84-\\tfrac12\\ln3=\\tfrac12\\ln28$. The field is undefined at the origin; any admissible path avoids it. No particular path is needed." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186672413 1080 "x(9\\ln x+\\frac12)" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186672413 1080 "x\\left(9\\ln x+\\frac12\\right)" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186697162 1074 "Let $g(x)=x^2-3x+3$ and rewrite the outer function as $F(u)=2u^{-1/2}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiate the outer and inner functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=2\\left(-\\frac12\\right)u^{-3/2}=-u^{-3/2}, \\\\\ng'(x)&=2x-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=-(x^2-3x+3)^{-3/2}(2x-3) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3-2x}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}(3-2x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing factor is $3-2x$. It includes both the inner derivative and the outer derivative's factor of $-1$.\n\nSince $x^2-3x+3=(x-\\frac32)^2+\\frac34>0$, this derivative is defined for every real $x$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186697162 1074 "Let $g(x)=x^2-3x+3$ and rewrite the outer function as $F(u)=2u^{-1/2}$, so $f(x)=F(g(x))$.\n\nDifferentiate the outer and inner functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF'(u)&=2\\left(-\\frac12\\right)u^{-3/2}=-u^{-3/2}, \\\\\ng'(x)&=2x-3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x)&=F'(g(x))g'(x) \\\\\n&=-(x^2-3x+3)^{-3/2}(2x-3) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3-2x}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}} \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{(x^2-3x+3)^{3/2}}(3-2x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe missing factor is $3-2x$. It includes both the inner derivative and the outer derivative's factor of $-1$.\n\nSince $x^2-3x+3=\\left(x-\\frac32\\right)^2+\\frac34>0$, this derivative is defined for every real $x$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186701572 1080 "(x+\\frac32)^2-\\frac72" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186701572 1080 "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac72" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186701573 1080 "(x+\\frac34)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186701573 1080 "\\left(x+\\frac34\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186701574 1080 "(x+\\frac32)^2+\\frac14" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186701574 1080 "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2+\\frac14" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186701580 1080 "(x+\\frac32)^2-2" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186701580 1080 "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-2" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186701581 1080 "(x+\\frac32)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186701581 1080 "\\left(x+\\frac32\\right)^2-\\frac{17}{4}" 13194139545766 17592186576411 true] [17592186741867 1074 "Let $u(x)=x^2-2x+2$ and $v(x)=x^2+x+1$. Then\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2x-2,\\qquad v'(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nExpand the two products in the numerator separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u'(x)v(x)&=(2x-2)(x^2+x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+2x^2+2x-2x^2-2x-2\\\\&=2x^3-2,\\\\u(x)v'(x)&=(x^2-2x+2)(2x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+x^2-4x^2-2x+4x+2\\\\&=2x^3-3x^2+2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract these products and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{(2x^3-2)-(2x^3-3x^2+2x+2)}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{2x^3-2-2x^3+3x^2-2x-2}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{3x^2-2x-4}{(x^2+x+1)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cubic terms cancel. The derivative is valid for every real $x$, since $x^2+x+1=(x+\\tfrac12)^2+\\tfrac34>0$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 false] [17592186741867 1074 "Let $u(x)=x^2-2x+2$ and $v(x)=x^2+x+1$. Then\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2x-2,\\qquad v'(x)=2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nExpand the two products in the numerator separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u'(x)v(x)&=(2x-2)(x^2+x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+2x^2+2x-2x^2-2x-2\\\\&=2x^3-2,\\\\u(x)v'(x)&=(x^2-2x+2)(2x+1)\\\\&=2x^3+x^2-4x^2-2x+4x+2\\\\&=2x^3-3x^2+2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract these products and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{(2x^3-2)-(2x^3-3x^2+2x+2)}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{2x^3-2-2x^3+3x^2-2x-2}{(x^2+x+1)^2}\\\\&=\\frac{3x^2-2x-4}{(x^2+x+1)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cubic terms cancel. The derivative is valid for every real $x$, since $x^2+x+1=\\left(x+\\tfrac12\\right)^2+\\tfrac34>0$." 13194139545766 17592186576411 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
